<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thérouanne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tarvenna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thérouanne is a village in the Pas-de-Calais in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the site of Tarvenna, the Roman capital of the Morini. It became the seat of a large bishopric in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and grew into a fortified town on the border between France and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. In 1553 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> captured it and razed it to the ground, and its diocese was divided.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/therouanne.jpg" alt="Thérouanne" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Tellberg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Th%C3%A9rouanneVillage.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Roman conquest the town was the capital of the Belgian tribe of the Morini, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> also made it the capital of the Civitas Morinorum district. The origin of its name is uncertain: the historian Malbracq derives it from a founder called Lucius Tauruannus, while others derive it from a phrase meaning the land of oats.</p><p>In the 7th century, probably around 639, Saint Audomar (Saint Omer) established the bishopric, whose diocese controlled a large part of the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The town lay in the county of Artois, which belonged to the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. Flemish forces burnt it in 1303 during the Franco-Flemish War. Through its control of prosperous cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>, the bishopric was able to build Thérouanne Cathedral, then the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The Emperor Maximilian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> captured the town from the French in 1513 after the Battle of the Spurs. In 1553 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> besieged it, then a French enclave in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, in revenge for a French defeat at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>. After he took the city he ordered it razed, the roads broken up, and the area ploughed and salted.</p><p>Only a small commune outside the walls, then called Saint-Martin-Outre-Eaux, was left standing, and probably around 1800 it took over the name Thérouanne. Part of the cathedral's portal went to Saint-Omer Cathedral, and a colossal statue of Christ sitting in judgement, le Grand Dieu de Thérouanne, is all that is left of it. The loss of the bishopric led to a reform of sees at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a>, and the diocese was split between those of Saint-Omer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Thérouanne is a commune in the Pas-de-Calais department, 10 kilometres west of Aire-sur-la-Lys and 13 kilometres south of Saint-Omer, on the river Lys. It lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a>. The ruins of the Abbey of St. Jean-du-Mont contain remnants of the Gothic cathedral, with a seventh-century episcopal group and a Carolingian building, and are a registered historical monument. The town appears in Hilary Mantel's novel Wolf Hall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thérouanne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Rome their capital was <b>Tarvenna</b> (Thérouanne), while Gesoriacum (Boulogne) became the base of the Roman Channel fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/morini">Morini</a></li></ul></section>
